The '''Sanyan Creole people''' ({{langx|kzi|ߞߌߙߌߦߏߏߟߎ|Kíríyóòlù}}) are an [[ethnic group]] of [[Sanyan]]. The Sanyan Creole people are [[W:Lineal descendant|descendants]] of freed [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Baliscan]], [[Kafto-Abyalans|Kafto-Abyalan]], and [[Sanyanian Liberated Kaftian|Liberated Kaftian]] slaves who settled in the [[Sanyan#Geography and climate|Sanyanian archipelago]] between 1787 and about 1839. Inline with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]], colonies were established across Sanyan as a place for [[W:Freedmen|freedmen]]. The first of these settlements was called [[Ògbódó Òminira]] ({{Literal translation|Maroon Town}}). Today, the Creoles are around 10 percent of the population of Sanyan.

The Creoles of Sanyan have varying degrees of Bayan-Fezzan [[W:Ancestry|ancestry]], similar to their [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Baliscan]] neighbors and sister ethnic group in [[Araucaria]]. In Ayorin, some of the settlers intermarried with Breislandic colonial residents and other Illypnians. Through the [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroons]], some Creoles probably also have indigenous [[Kurupiranga|Indigenous Kurupiranga]] ancestry. The mingling of newly freed black and [[W:Multiracial people|racially-mixed]] [[Araucarians|Araucarians/Maroons]] from the '[[W:New World|New World]]' with [[Liberated Kaftians in Sanyan|Liberated Kaftians]] – such as the [[W:Akan people|Sikani]], [[W:Wolof people|Waajur]], [[W:Bambara people|Wulani]], [[W:Igbo people|Ekwe]] and [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]] – over several generations in the late 18th and early 19th centuries, led to the eventual [[W:Creole peoples|formation of a Creole ethnicity]].

